1a. gAtoms compiler-warning cleanup in src/igraph/vendor/
--------------------------------------------------------

  Scope:      src/igraph/vendor/f2c/ and src/igraph/vendor/lapack/.
              51 files, 150 changed source lines. Of those files 13 are inside
              the compiled subset (section 1c), among them vendor/f2c/wref.c,
              wsfe.c, open.c and endfile.c and vendor/lapack/dgemv.c, dlascl.c,
              lsame.c and dtrevc.c.
  Purpose:    allow a clean `R CMD check --as-cran`. R's check treats certain
              GCC diagnostics as "significant warnings" and fails the package
              with a WARNING when any appear during compilation. Compiled as the
              full 700-object library, the pristine igraph 1.0.1 sources emit 101
              such diagnostics:

                56  "suggest parentheses around '&&' within '||'"
                44  "suggest parentheses around <arithmetic> inside '<</>>'"
                    or "... in operand of '|'"
                 1  "cast from pointer to integer of different size"
                    [-Wpointer-to-int-cast]

  The cleanup consists of two purely mechanical, behaviour-preserving changes:

    (a) Explicit parentheses, and nothing else. Every edit inserts only "(" and
        ")". No token is added, removed, reordered or rewritten, so operator
        precedence, evaluation order, short-circuit behaviour, assignment side
        effects, control flow and the resulting values are all unchanged --
        the parentheses only spell out the grouping the compiler already
        applied. Representative cases:

          if (k = getnum(chp, x1))          ->  if ((k = getnum(chp, x1)))
          while (ch = *sc++)                ->  while ((ch = *sc++))
          if (*ldz < 1 || wantz && *ldz < *n)
                                            ->  if (*ldz < 1 || (wantz && *ldz < *n))
          i__1 = n0 - 3 << 2;               ->  i__1 = (n0 - 3) << 2;
          j4 = (*n0 - 2 << 2) - *pp;        ->  j4 = ((*n0 - 2) << 2) - *pp;
          x >> LONGBITS - b                 ->  x >> (LONGBITS - b)

        Files (f2c):   dfe.c due.c endfile.c iio.c inquire.c lbitbits.c
                       lread.c lwrite.c open.c rdfmt.c rsfe.c rsne.c sue.c
                       wref.c wsfe.c wsle.c wsne.c
        Files (lapack): dgeev.c dgeevx.c dgemm.c dgemv.c dhseqr.c dlaexc.c
                       dlaqr0.c dlaqr4.c dlarrd.c dlarre.c dlarrf.c dlascl.c
                       dlasq2.c dlasq3.c dlasq5.c dlasq6.c dorm2l.c dorm2r.c
                       dormql.c dormqr.c dstebz.c dstemr.c dsteqr.c dsyevr.c
                       dsymv.c dsyr2k.c dsyrk.c dtrevc.c dtrexc.c dtrsen.c
                       dtrsna.c ilaenv.c lsame.c

    (b) One pointer-width cast in vendor/f2c/signal_.c. The function returns the
        previous signal handler (a function pointer) in f2c's legacy 32-bit
        `ftnint` return type. The single-step cast is split into
          (intptr_t)(void *)signal(sig, proc)
        so the pointer is first widened to its own width and only then narrowed
        to ftnint. This is behaviour preserving: the original code also narrowed
        straight to ftnint, so no truncation is added or removed; the
        intermediate type only makes the existing narrowing explicit and keeps
        the conversion portable. `signal_` is not declared or called anywhere in
        igraph, so no result is consumed.

  No algorithm, numerical result, control flow, API or object manifest is
  changed. No `-Wno-*` suppression flag and no `#pragma GCC diagnostic` is used
  anywhere, and no warning is silenced rather than fixed. The cleanup is applied
  to the vendored sources as shipped; of those files only the subset listed in
  src/Makevars.igraph is actually compiled (see section 1c), and that subset
  compiles with no significant diagnostic under GCC 13 and GCC 15 in both C11
  and C23 modes.

  A replayable patch against the pristine release is kept at
  tools/patches/igraph-1.0.1-f2c-cran-warnings.patch (tools/ is excluded from
  the R source package).


1b. gAtoms-added configuration headers, forwarding header and portability edits
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  igraph 1.0.x moved its public headers directly into include/. gAtoms sources
  include <igraph/igraph.h>, so a single-line forwarding header is provided:

    src/igraph/include/igraph/igraph.h       contains only
                                             #include "../igraph.h"

  It declares nothing, defines no macro and changes no behaviour; it exists
  purely so the include path <igraph/igraph.h> resolves.

  Two of the five configuration headers igraph's build generates are stored in a
  portable form rather than verbatim, because a single copy has to serve Windows,
  Linux and macOS and CMake would otherwise have to run at install time (it does
  not):

    src/igraph/include/igraph_export.h
        CMake always writes IGRAPH_DEPRECATED as __declspec(deprecated), the MSVC
        spelling, which GCC and Clang reject on Linux and macOS. The macro is
        selected per compiler instead: __declspec for MSVC, __attribute__
        ((deprecated)) for GCC and Clang, nothing for an unknown compiler. The
        deprecation is still a real compiler attribute everywhere; it is never
        defined away to empty just to silence a diagnostic. Nothing else in the
        file differs from the generated output.

    src/igraph/include/config.h
        Upstream this file records the results of CMake feature probes on the
        build machine. The probes are expressed as compile-time platform tests
        instead. The Windows branch reproduces the CMake output for the bundled
        copy token for token, so the Windows build is unchanged; the other branch
        marks the Microsoft-only facilities as absent, which is what a POSIX host
        reports. The entries that matter are HAVE__UMUL128 (its presence makes
        src/random/random.c include <intrin.h>, which Linux and macOS do not
        have), HAVE__POPCNT*/HAVE__BITSCAN* (MSVC intrinsics), HAVE__STRICMP and
        HAVE__STRNICMP (replaced on POSIX by the HAVE_STRCASECMP and
        HAVE_STRNCASECMP probes, which are set) and the TLS keyword. Entries that
        record gAtoms' own build choices rather than a platform probe
        (HAVE_INFOMAP, INTERNAL_*) are identical on every platform.

  The other three generated headers (igraph_config.h, igraph_threading.h,
  igraph_version.h) are stored verbatim; they contain no platform-specific
  construct. Section 3 describes the configuration they were produced from.


1c. Which igraph translation units gAtoms compiles
-------------------------------------------------

  The package ships the whole igraph source tree but compiles only the part the
  gAtoms shared object needs: 189 of the 700 translation units igraph's own
  CMake build produces. Nothing is deleted from src/igraph; the selection is
  purely the object list in src/Makevars.igraph.

  The list was derived, not guessed. Each of the 700 objects was built once and
  its global symbols extracted with nm; the undefined symbols of gAtoms' own
  objects (decom_h.o, r_interface.o) were then resolved transitively against the
  symbol->provider map. That closure is 188 objects and leaves no igraph symbol
  unresolved. Every object in it is a C translation unit, so no kept object can
  refer to a mangled C++ name and C symbol matching is exact on every platform.
  The one call that sits in an inactive conditional branch on Windows
  (vendor/f2c/wref.c -> signbit_f2c) was found by scanning the kept sources for
  identifiers naming symbols that only excluded objects define; its provider is
  included, which brings the total to 189. That makes the manifest a superset of
  the closure on every platform rather than a Windows-only minimum.

  What is not compiled, because nothing in the closure reaches it: the whole
  file-reading layer and its Bison parsers, the layout algorithms, community
  detection, most centrality and path routines, the random graph generators,
  Qhull, Infomap, plfit, PCG, GMP, and 130 of the 153 f2c runtime units. The
  bundled LAPACK/ARPACK and CSparse clusters are compiled because
  igraph_get_edgelist() reaches src/core/sparsemat.c, which uses both.

  The five Bison-generated parsers and vendor/f2c/uninit.c, which GCC 14 reports
  significant diagnostics for, are outside that set and are therefore never
  compiled; no patch to them is needed.


2. Third-party components bundled inside igraph
-----------------------------------------------

igraph itself vendors several independent libraries. The copies below are
distributed as part of igraph and are covered by their own licenses, whose
texts are collected in inst/licenses/.

  vendor/cs         CSparse (Tim Davis)             LGPL-2.1-or-later
  vendor/f2c        f2c runtime (netlib)            BSD-style, see the README
  vendor/lapack     BLAS / LAPACK / ARPACK (netlib) BSD-3-clause
  vendor/mini-gmp   mini-gmp (GNU)                  LGPL-3.0-or-later / GPL-2.0+
  vendor/pcg        PCG random number generator     Apache-2.0
  vendor/plfit      plfit (Clauset et al.)          MIT
  vendor/qhull      Qhull (Brad Barber)             Qhull custom (MIT-like)
  vendor/infomap    Infomap (Rosvall/Edler)         GPL-3.0-or-later

  License texts: inst/licenses/lgpl-2.1.txt, lgpl-3.0.txt, gpl-3.0.txt,
  Licence_CeCILL-B_V1-en.txt, Licence_CeCILL-B_V1-fr.txt,
  vendor-cs-License.txt, vendor-f2c-README.txt, vendor-pcg-LICENSE.txt.
  Each vendored directory also keeps its own license file in place under
  src/igraph/vendor/.

  The GNU Linear Programming Kit (GLPK) is NOT included: gAtoms does not use
  any GLPK functionality, and igraph is compiled here with GLPK support
  disabled (IGRAPH_GLPK_SUPPORT=OFF). This also avoids pulling in a GPL-3.0-only
  component. No GLPK object is part of the manifest.


3. Build configuration of the bundled copy
------------------------------------------

The bundled igraph was configured offline, once, with igraph's own CMake
build system (CMake is a development-time tool only and is not used when
installing gAtoms):

  BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF            static library
  IGRAPH_GLPK_SUPPORT=OFF          no GLPK
  IGRAPH_OPENMP_SUPPORT=OFF        no OpenMP runtime dependency
  no libxml2                       no XML parser dependency
  vendored ARPACK                  internal ARPACK
  vendored BLAS                    internal BLAS
  vendored LAPACK                  internal LAPACK
  vendored mini-GMP                internal GMP
  vendored plfit                   internal plfit
  vendored Qhull                   internal Qhull
  vendored Infomap                 internal Infomap

The resulting configuration headers (config.h, igraph_config.h,
igraph_export.h, igraph_threading.h, igraph_version.h) are stored in
src/igraph/include/. Three of them are kept exactly as generated and two are
made platform-portable, as described in section 1b; CMake itself is never run
when installing gAtoms. That CMake run builds all 700 translation units; the
subset gAtoms compiles, and the include paths and compile definitions it uses,
are recorded in src/Makevars.igraph, which src/Makevars and src/Makevars.win
both follow. Upgrading igraph therefore means repeating that one offline CMake
run, re-deriving the subset from it (section 1c) and refreshing those files;
nothing about it happens at install time.


